<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 73 0 R/T(The same call, p.calculatePoints\(20, 10, 5, true\), returns different values depending on the real type of the object behind the reference. The program output in Section 6 shows this directly: a ProPlayer gets 82.5 points and a CasualPlayer gets 50.0 for identical statistics. Because the functions are virtual, the choice is made at run time. The tournament code contains no if on the player type, so a new category \(for example a StreamerPlayer\) could be added without changing Match or Tournament.)/E(The same call, p.calculatePoints\(20, 10, 5, true\), returns different values depending on the real type of the object behind the reference. The program output in Section 6 shows this directly: a ProPlayer gets 82.5 points and a CasualPlayer gets 50.0 for identical statistics. Because the functions are virtual, the choice is made at run time. The tournament code contains no if on the player type, so a new category \(for example a StreamerPlayer\) could be added without changing Match or Tournament.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[63]>>
